{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Discovered while proving the MCP OOM root cause on witted-invisible-roan (9ea3-7d07-ea55-4496). Shares that bug's root cause — whole-store materialization — but is a DIFFERENT and worse retention profile, and its contribution was NOT separately measured, so it is filed rather than folded into that fix.\n\n## Observed\n\n`build_plan_relation_snapshot` calls `reduce_all_tickets` with all three filters OFF — the MAXIMAL compile, including verbose `session_log` and `code_review` bodies:\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/relation_snapshot.py:257-262` — `exclude_archived=False, exclude_deleted=False, exclude_session_logs=False`.\n- The result is fanned into `states: dict[str, dict]` (`relation_snapshot.py:268`) and returned as `PlanRelationSnapshot.ticket_states_by_id` (frozen dataclass field, `relation_snapshot.py:65`).\n- That snapshot is held by `PlanReviewGeneration.relation_snapshot` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py:48`) and threaded through the whole multi-pass review, captured by the fan-out closures (`plan_review/pass1.py:434`, `plan_review/container_stage.py:259`, `plan_review/generation.py:154`).\n\n## Why this is worse than the read-path case\n\nOn the read path the whole-store compile is a TRANSIENT — measured 243.4 MB on this store (8,879 tickets), released as soon as the call returns. Here it is **retained for the full duration of a plan review**, which `AGENTS.md` documents as 15-20 minutes. Concurrent reviews therefore stack: N overlapping reviews hold N copies simultaneously, and on the 7.6 GiB box the MCP container shares the host with Gerrit, the review-bot and opcert.\n\nMeasured on the real store for the equivalent compile: 243.4 MB per snapshot held. Ten to nineteen overlapping reviews reproduces the reported 368 MiB -> 4.5-4.8 GiB curve on its own, which is consistent with the reported busy window (\"six changes under review, several agents pushing patchsets\").\n\n## Why it is not fixed by the 9ea3 change\n\nThe fix landing on 9ea3 adds an `omit_fields` projection applied INSIDE `reduce_all_tickets`' per-ticket loop, and uses it from `list_states` and `find_ready_tickets`. It deliberately does NOT touch this call site: unlike the discovery reads, the plan reviewer may genuinely consume the bodies and signature material of related tickets, so which fields (if any) are droppable here needs its own analysis and its own measurement.\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\nUse the documented best-practice default for this small design fork: retain only the subject and\nits directly material related tickets in the long-lived `PlanRelationSnapshot.ticket_states_by_id`\nmap, and drop unrelated whole-store states before the snapshot is returned. This mirrors common\nactive OSS compiler/indexer practice: build a broad transient index when discovery needs it, then\nstore only the minimal dependency slice in the durable/long-lived object handed to later phases.\nIt preserves review behavior because the broad `states` map remains available inside\n`collect_plan_relation_snapshot` while resolving aliases, enumerating children/prerequisites, and\ncomputing material pins; only the returned retention payload changes.\n\nConsumer/field inventory to preserve:\n\n- `generation.from_snapshot` reads `subject_state`, `child_ids`, `related_material`, and\n  `ticket_store_revision`; it carries `relation_snapshot` but does not read unrelated rows from\n  `ticket_states_by_id`.\n- `plan_review.__init__` builds prerequisite review blocks from\n  `ticket_states_by_id[prerequisite_id].title` and `.description`.\n- `production_batch_runner.ProductionBatchRunner.run` consumes preloaded relation snapshots for\n  prerequisite focused review and reads `ticket_states_by_id[prerequisite_id].description`.\n- signing/resign/sidecar paths inspect the snapshot object or its related-material pins; they do\n  not require unrelated ticket states.\n\nFile impact is limited to the relation snapshot collection path and focused unit tests that pin\nretention scaling and consumer compatibility.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: every consumer of `PlanRelationSnapshot.ticket_states_by_id` is enumerated with citations,\n      and the set of fields each actually reads is recorded.\n- [ ] AC2: peak and retained memory of a plan-review generation is measured on a real-sized store,\n      before and after, under comparable load; the contribution of THIS site is established\n      separately rather than bundled with the 9ea3 read-path fix.\n- [ ] AC3: a held-out regression test asserts the retained snapshot does not scale with whole-store\n      size, verified RED before the fix and RED again when the fix is perturbed.\n- [ ] AC4: `rebar review-plan` verdicts are unchanged; fixture review/prompt consumers keep the same\n      subject, child, prerequisite, and material-pin inputs before and after.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788821962058838001,"uuid":"d57f2b26-05f2-46d0-a208-0951560d8d62"}